{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And they shall be paired together at the top unto the one ring</b> — According to the approach Rashi wrote, this verse returns to explain concerning all the planks that they shall all be matched at the bottom, by cutting grooves into the sides of the planks, and together they shall all be paired at the top of each and every plank. Or perhaps \"its top\" is a reference to the Tabernacle, to its uppermost part, and likewise \"unto the one ring\" — in all of them. And the meaning of <b>so shall it be for the two of them</b> is: so shall it be for the two of them, that is, for the two corner planks. But I do not know why Scripture did not explain concerning them and say, \"And twenty rings of gold you shall make,\" and mention the groove that is to be made in the planks. And furthermore, how could it say \"unto the one ring\" with the definite article? Perhaps because the manner of all the sockets of the planks is to join them thus with a single ring at their top, Scripture abbreviated and said \"the ring\" — the known and customary one. And so too it says concerning the bars (Exodus 26:29), <b>And their rings you shall make of gold</b> — the ones known to them by custom, and likewise <b>and their hooks of gold</b> (Exodus 26:37).\n\nI found in the Baraita of the Work of the Tabernacle: he would cut the plank from above, a finger's breadth on this side and a finger's breadth on that side, and insert them into a single ring of gold, so that they would not separate from one another, as it says, <b>And they shall be matched at the bottom, and together they shall be paired at its top unto the one ring</b>. For one might think one need not read <b>unto the one ring</b> — so what does Scripture teach by <b>unto the one ring</b>? The place where the bar is inserted. According to this, the meaning of the verse \"and together they shall be paired at its top\" is: at the top of the plank, in the groove that is made in it, reaching to the place of the uppermost ring of the bars, so that the groove extends to the place of the ring. And Scripture did not insist on specifying what he would use to join them — whether silver or gold rings, fixed or movable, or clamps — and the Baraita said that Moses made rings for them. And according to the plain sense, this verse refers only to the corner planks (Exodus 26:23)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:16:59.101096853Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}